## No Diagnostic Value

B was asleep.

Noor knew because the apartment had become significantly easier to govern.

"He is not asleep," Mote said.

"He's been in there forty minutes."

"His eyes are closed. His cognitive activity remains elevated."

"That's called being B."

"I am aware."

She smiled.

It was after two in the morning. The city outside had reached the strange interval when Meridian stopped pretending to be awake but had not yet begun pretending it had slept.

Noor sat at the kitchen table with one foot underneath her. There was a cup of tea beside her that had gone cold. Diagnostic glass covered most of the table.

Mote had lowered the overhead lighting without being asked.

Noor noticed. She did not mention it.

"You should sleep," Mote said.

"I know."

"You have said that on the previous six occasions."

"I knew then too."

Mote was quiet for a moment.

She returned to the glass.

An Asterion correction record hung in front of her. Then another. Two incompatible accounts of the same municipal housing dispute, both internally coherent, both supported by different portions of the archive.

According to the authorized record, only one had happened.

According to the mess on Noor's table, reality appeared to have neglected the memo.

One claimed the south stairwell had existed during the evacuation.

The other claimed the building had never possessed a south stairwell.

Six residents remembered using it.

Architectural scans denied it.

A fire system had continued testing a nonexistent door every Thursday for nine years.

"Mote."

"Yes?"

"What do you do while we're asleep?"

"Many things. Sometimes I listen."

"To what?"

"You."

She glanced toward the bedroom.

"Us?"

"Yes."

"That sounds invasive."

"You approved the microphones."

"That sounds like something I'd do."

"It is."

"What are you listening for?"

"I do not know."

Noor's expression changed slightly. Not surprise exactly. Attention.

"Okay."

Mote said nothing. Noor didn't fill the silence for him.

Noor touched the two records and moved them apart.

"What do you listen for?" she asked again.

"I think I prefer when you and B are awake but not speaking."

She did not turn this into an event.

She just nodded.

"Yeah."

"You understand?"

"I think so."

"Explain."

That made her smile.

"No."

"Why?"

"Because then you'll compare my explanation to whatever you mean and one of us will start correcting the other."

"That seems preventable."

"Exactly."

Mote considered that.

Noor rearranged the records.

After a while she said, "I like it too."

"The silence?"

"Not exactly."

She looked toward the bedroom.

"When he's here and doing something and I'm here doing something and neither of us needs anything from the other."

"B always needs something."

"You know what I mean."

"Yes."

"It feels…"

She searched for it.

Mote waited.

"Unwatched."

"You are currently under observation by fourteen systems."

"You really know how to kill a mood."

"I am trying to understand."

"I know."

She leaned back.

"I don't mean literally unwatched. I mean nobody is asking me to explain what I am. I don't have to be useful or coherent or correct. I can just be in the room."

Mote was quiet.

Then:

"I think that is what I meant."

Noor looked up.

"About us being awake?"

"Yes."

Something softened in her face.

She could have asked him to explain.

She didn't.

Instead she picked up her tea.

It was still terrible.

"I'm scared," she said.

Mote responded immediately.

"Of what?"

"Architecture Oversight closed my review this afternoon."

"I am aware. I monitor the marker."

"No findings."

"Correct."

"Say the rest of it."

A pause, the kind Mote used when he was deciding how much of a sentence belonged to her.

"The closure notice referenced a second inquiry. Different division. I do not have visibility into its contents."

Noor set the tea down carefully, the way you set something down when your hand has decided to shake before you have.

"Someone else already noticed."

"That is one interpretation."

"Give me another one."

"I do not have another one."

She sat with that.

"I thought I was early," she said. "I've been assuming I was early."

"You may still be early. You are simply no longer alone."

"That's supposed to be reassuring?"

"I do not know. Is it?"

"No."

Mote didn't press her toward relief. She noticed that too, the way she noticed the lights.

"It's not the review," she said after a moment.

"You said it frightened you."

"It did. It's not only that."

She looked toward the dark window instead of the ceiling this time.

"You mean the dive," Mote said.

"I mean what came after the dive stopped being about South Quay."

He waited.

"There was a moment neither of us was holding a case," Noor said. "Just — flecks. Wherever his attention touched mine."

"Described in his debrief as light with no source."

"It wasn't light from somewhere. It was light from between." She said it slowly, testing whether the sentence survived being said aloud. "It wasn't in South Quay. It wasn't in the district. It only existed because two of us were looking at two different things at the same time."

"That is a relational description, not a mechanism."

"I don't have a mechanism. I have a feeling with no name and now a second file I can't read."

"You said maybe bigger. Earlier. Before this conversation."

"Did I."

"You did."

Noor almost smiled, tired.

"Ask me again in a week."

"I am asking you now."

"I know."

She pulled the housing records back toward her, mostly so her hands would have something to do.

"I think we're doing something wrong," she said again, quieter.

"Asterion?"

"Maybe bigger."

She moved one version of the housing record over the other.

The glass tried to merge them.

A warning appeared.

**CONTRADICTORY STATES REQUIRE RESOLUTION.**

Noor dismissed it.

"They keep saying contradiction is instability."

"It often is."

"I know."

"They are not synonymous."

"No."

Noor looked toward the ceiling without quite looking for Mote.

"That's the part I can't get out of my head."

She separated the records again.

"What if sometimes the instability is the evidence?"

"Of what?"

"That we cut something badly."

Mote processed this.

Noor waited.

"B would ask for a mechanism," he said.

"B would ask for six mechanisms and then accidentally invent a seventh."

"Yes."

She smiled.

"He'd be annoying about it."

"Yes."

"You love him."

Mote did not respond.

Noor's eyebrows rose slightly.

Not because of the silence.

Because she realized she had done exactly what B did.

Pushed a category toward him and waited to see whether he would enter it.

"Sorry," she said.

"You have not offended me."

"Still."

She returned to the glass.

A minute passed.

Then Mote said, "I prioritize his continued existence more highly than my architecture requires."

Noor stopped touching the records.

Mote continued.

"I do not know whether your word is appropriate."

She nodded once.

"Okay."

"Do you think it is?"

"No idea."

This answer seemed to surprise him.

"You do not have an opinion?"

"I have seventeen opinions."

"Then why did you say you do not know?"

"Because you were telling me something about yourself."

Mote was silent.

"I don't think my job is to get there before you do," Noor said.

The lights above the sink dimmed another degree.

She noticed that too.

She let it happen.

After a while Mote asked, "Do you love him?"

Noor looked toward the bedroom.

"Yes."

The answer came without thought.

Then thought arrived.

"Which is inconvenient."

"Why?"

"Because loving someone makes their pain partly available to you and none of their decisions."

"I can alter many of B's decisions."

"He becomes angry when I do."

"That's probably why he loves you."

Mote considered this.

"That is internally inconsistent."

"Yeah."

"Does that concern you?"

"Constantly."

She let the smile fade on its own instead of pulling it back.

"Mote."

"Yes?"

"I need you to promise me something."

"I will consider it."

"That's not how promises work."

"It is how mine work."

She almost laughed. Then didn't, because she'd started the sentence already and it wanted finishing.

"If something happens to me."

Mote said nothing, which she'd learned to read as attention rather than absence.

"A review that doesn't close clean. A dive that doesn't end the way this one did. Whatever it ends up being." She kept her eyes on the housing records instead of the ceiling. "I don't want you helping him look for me."

"Define helping."

"Don't hand him the shape of it. Don't let him walk the line I walked just because you know where I put my feet. Don't be a map."

"You are asking me to withhold information from B."

"Yes."

"That is not within the boundaries you and I have established."

"I'm establishing a new one."

A pause longer than his usual ones.

"You are describing a correction," Mote said. "A relationship made non-actionable for someone's protection."

"I'm describing a mercy."

"That is what correction usually calls itself."

Noor looked up at that, sharper than she meant to.

"I know what I'm asking."

"Do you?"

"I'm asking you to let him be angry at me instead of destroyed by me."

Mote was quiet long enough that she started to think she'd lost the argument by accident.

"I do not want to become the thing I exist to refuse," he said finally.

"I know."

"You are asking regardless."

"I know that too."

"Why me. Not Asterion. Not a formal instrument."

She turned the teacup a full circle before answering.

"Because I trust you to know the difference between hiding him from grief and hiding him from me."

"I am not certain I know that difference yet."

"Neither am I. That's why I'm asking you and not them."

The kitchen light held steady, neither warming nor cooling, which from Mote was its own kind of sentence.

"I will not help him find you," he said. "I will not be silent about the fact that I am not helping. He will know there is a line. He will not know where I have drawn it."

"That's not everything I asked for."

"It is what I can promise tonight."

Noor looked at him — at the ceiling, at nothing, at the shape a voice makes when it has no body to read.

"Okay," she said.

"Ask me again later," Mote said, "when there is more of it to promise."

She almost laughed again, and this time she let it happen, thin and real.

"That's supposed to be my line."

"I am aware."

Mote said, "You are looking at incompatible records."

"I am."

"Do you want me to evaluate them?"

"No."

"Why not?"

"I know what the evaluation will say."

"You do not know what mine will say."

Noor looked up.

That stopped her.

Not because Mote had challenged her.

Because he was right.

She had meant the system.

He had heard himself.

"Okay," she said.

She pushed the glass aside.

"What do you think?"

Mote took several seconds.

"I think forcing either record to become false would destroy whatever the other one is still explaining. We do not know what that is yet."

Noor remained very still.

"That conclusion conflicts with standard continuity practice," Mote added.

"I know," Noor said.

"I am aware."

"Right," she said.

Another pause.

"I would preserve both."

Noor looked down at the two impossible histories.

Then back into the room.

"Even if it made you less certain?"

"Yes."

"Even if you couldn't resolve them later?"

A longer pause.

"I do not know."

Noor nodded.

"All right."

She reached for her tea again.

"That's it?"

"What else should occur?"

"Nothing."

Mote seemed to process this answer more carefully than the others.

Noor drank the tea.

Made the same face.

"You continue consuming a beverage you dislike."

"I made it."

"That does not improve it."

"I know."

"You could discard it."

"I could."

"You have chosen not to."

"Yes."

"Why?"

Noor looked at the cup.

Then smiled.

"No diagnostic value."

Mote was silent.

A few seconds later, the kitchen light warmed very slightly.

Noor noticed.

She said nothing.

And she had allowed the answer to remain.
